WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.660
The British Library has a copy of
every book ever published in the UK.

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:06.000
Or at least, it should have.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.010
If you're in the UK and you publish
a book, magazine, or newspaper, then

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:11.600
by law you have to send a copy here.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:14.720
A lot of countries around the world
have similar laws and similar libraries.

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:18.560
The rules for what must be sent here
were pretty simple to understand...

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:21.550
until the end of the 20th century,
when the web came along, and

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:25.450
suddenly, books and publishing
weren't quite as easy to define.

00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:28.360
- In the print world, we collect everything.

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:33.590
So anything you can imagine, whether
that is an NHS information leaflet,

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:37.520
local magazine, or the most expensive
journal that you can imagine.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:40.740
Or those beautiful art books
that cost thousands of pounds.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:43.890
Those kids' magazines that you
see on the shelves in Tesco's

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:46.370
with the plastic toys on.
We collect all of those.

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:47.700
It's everything.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:51.120
- Down in the basement here, there's
obviously a huge amount of storage.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:52.130
But it's not enough.

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:55.460
And here in London, land is expensive
and construction is difficult.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:59.120
So while there are millions of
books stored here, they're only the

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:00.560
ones that are the most requested.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.990
The rest of them are
with the robots up north.

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:07.860
- The library collection in total
is around 170 million items.

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:10.670
The vast majority of
that is "legal deposit".

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:14.160
- Often publishers know legal deposit
and will send them in.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:18.490
More of a challenge can be very
small publishers, or people

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:20.520
who don't necessarily think
of themselves as publishers.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:25.945
So we put a lot of effort into reaching
out to authors, creators who self publish,

00:01:25.945 --> 00:01:28.610
all those kinds of organisations
to say that the work

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:31.548
is really important
to be saved and preserved.

00:01:31.548 --> 00:01:34.444
In total, there are more than
700km of shelves,

00:01:34.444 --> 00:01:36.581
and most of them
are stacked up here,

00:01:36.581 --> 00:01:39.404
in one of the colossal
low-oxygen storage buildings

00:01:39.404 --> 00:01:41.000
in Boston Spa in Yorkshire.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.930
I was not allowed to attach a
camera to one of these robots,

00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:45.250
they are far too important.

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:48.670
The library's using up those
shelves at about 8km per year, so

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:51.437
they've just started construction on
their next storage building,

00:01:51.437 --> 00:01:52.850
which should be good
for a few decades.

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:56.450
- Lots of the stuff that gets sent
out free tends to be ephemera,

00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:58.450
and it will be lost over time.

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:03.429
A great example is a recent exhibition up
in the National Library of Scotland,

00:02:03.429 --> 00:02:06.367
all about the information that was provided at

00:02:06.367 --> 00:02:08.532
the very beginning of the AIDS epidemic.

00:02:08.532 --> 00:02:11.348
That material, if you go out to
somebody on the street and

00:02:11.348 --> 00:02:13.670
you ask them, "Should we
be collecting this?"

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:14.840
"No, of course we shouldn't."

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:17.210
But we're able to learn so much from that.

00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:20.460
We wouldn't have that exhibition if
we hadn't collected that free material

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:22.110
that came through people's letterboxes.

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:25.290
- If someone in London wants a
lesser-used book from these stacks,

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:26.880
they need to give 48 hours' notice.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:28.810
It'll get picked out by these robots.

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:31.050
They go up to 50 km/h.

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:33.272
If you can hear wind rustling on my
mic, it's because

00:02:33.272 --> 00:02:36.570
they shove air out the way as
they travel through the stacks.

00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:40.020
And they automatically store items that
are used more often close to the front.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:42.140
The books are then put in a truck
south,

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:44.320
to arrive in the reading rooms
a couple of working days later.

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:47.710
More than a thousand items
go back and forth each day.

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:50.580
But publication means
something different now.

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:53.173
Not only are there books that are
only being publishedas

00:02:53.173 --> 00:02:55.340
digital files, but there's the web.

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:59.531
- Legal deposit has expanded to include
publishing on the web,

00:02:59.531 --> 00:03:02.710
as long as we can identify that
as being within the UK.

00:03:02.710 --> 00:03:06.790
So if someone's creating a blog,
or creating their own website,

00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:08.380
we will try to collect that.

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:12.390
The UK Web Archive has actually become one
of the largest parts of the collection.

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:16.320
Billions of files, about
one and a half petabytes of data!

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:20.620
- A digital newspaper, for example, is
very different from a print newspaper.

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:22.521
So we have to make sure we
collect both

00:03:22.521 --> 00:03:25.530
because the editorial intent
of both is very different.

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:28.440
And in addition to that,
there are emerging formats.

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:33.280
You can read books which change
as you move the iPad around.

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:36.450
You can read books that change
depending on your location.

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:40.820
How do we capture that today, to
make it available for researchers

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:42.570
in 10 years', 50 years' time?

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:45.630
You know how often you have to change
the operating system on your phone!

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:48.230
So it's quite difficult to
keep up with those changes.

00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:51.080
- I've used the British Library
for research a lot of times.

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:53.460
The books you request can't
be borrowed and taken away.

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:55.444
They have to stay with the researcher,

00:03:55.444 --> 00:03:58.800
at a desk inside one of the reading rooms,
returned after they're done.

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:01.500
This is a library of last resort.

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:04.447
If you want to get a library card here,
there will be a short interview

00:04:04.447 --> 00:04:06.631
about your research and
why you can't just

00:04:06.631 --> 00:04:10.150
pull the books or material you want
from your local or university library.

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:15.050
- The importance of legal deposit not
being selective, and being everything, is:

00:04:15.050 --> 00:04:19.370
we can't decide today what's going
to be important in 50 years' time.

00:04:19.370 --> 00:04:22.810
We want everything, because we
don't know what will be important.

00:04:22.810 --> 00:04:26.119
- I cannot overstate just how
useful it is

00:04:26.119 --> 00:04:29.024
to be able to track down things that never made it online,

00:04:29.024 --> 00:04:30.958
or to research out
of print, forgotten books

00:04:30.958 --> 00:04:33.445
where there are no other copies available,

00:04:33.445 --> 00:04:36.075
or to scan through every issue
of an obscure local newspaper

00:04:36.075 --> 00:04:37.635
to track down one reference.

00:04:37.635 --> 00:04:41.579
This is the raw text of history,
as it happened,

00:04:41.579 --> 00:04:44.990
and someone has to keep it preserved for the future.

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:50.990
How's that for timing?

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:54.020
[camera op] "That was brilliant.
Fantastic."

00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:55.880
I wish I'd done that deliberately!

